**This is a Google penalty and traffic drop recovery service run by me as an independent UK SEO consultant. It is for sites that lost organic traffic after a Google core update, a manual action or a site migration. You start with a free 20-minute diagnosis. A fuller investigation runs as my £495 fixed audit. It ends with a written recovery plan ranked by what to fix first.**

Most traffic drops get treated as a penalty. The first instinct is to change everything at once. That usually makes the drop harder to read. Recovery starts with finding the actual cause. A manual action, a core update, a technical fault and a migration each need a different fix.

{% cta heading="Lost traffic and not sure why?" text="Book the free 20-minute diagnosis. Send me the site and the date the drop started, and I will tell you what I would check first." /%}

## What I check first for each type of drop

Each type of drop has its own first check and its own route to recovery.

| Your situation | What I check first | Likely route |
|---|---|---|
| **Manual action** in Search Console | The Manual Actions report, the policy named and the pages affected | Fix the stated violation and send a reconsideration request |
| **Core update drop** | Drop date against Google's confirmed update dates, which pages and queries fell, and what now ranks instead | Improve the pages that fell and wait for Google to reassess |
| **Technical drop** | Indexing report, noindex and canonical rules, robots.txt, server errors and sitemaps | Fix the fault and request recrawling of key pages |
| **Migration drop** | Old-to-new redirect map, lost URLs, changed templates and internal links | Repair redirects and missing pages |

The diagnosis tells you which row you are in. Many drops are a mix. A common example is a migration that went live in the same month as a core update.

