## What is a Google algorithm update?

A Google algorithm update is a change to the systems Google uses to rank search results. Google makes many small changes each year and several larger "core updates". Core updates do not target individual sites. They change how Google assesses content across the web. Some sites rise and others fall.

## What is a Google penalty?

"Penalty" is used for two different things. The recovery route differs for each.

**Manual actions** are applied by human reviewers at Google when a site breaks [Google's spam policies](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies). Common causes are unnatural links, thin affiliate content, cloaking, hidden text and user-generated spam. They show in Google Search Console under Security & Manual Actions > Manual Actions. The report names the problem.

**Algorithmic drops** are not penalties in the strict sense. They are ranking changes after Google's systems reassess your site. Google sends no notification. That makes them harder to diagnose.

There is no manual action when the Manual Actions report says "No issues detected". That does not prove an algorithm change caused the drop. Technical faults, lost demand, tracking changes and stronger competitors all look the same on a traffic graph. I rule those out first with [Google's guidance on debugging traffic drops](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/monitor-debug/debugging-search-traffic-drops).

## How the recovery process works

{% process %}
{% step title="Confirm the cause" detail="Check Manual Actions, indexing and tracking. Compare the drop date with Google's confirmed update history. Check whether impressions fell (rankings) or only clicks (click-through rate)." /%}
{% step title="Find what fell and why" detail="Identify which pages and queries lost ground and look for the pattern. Informational losses often point to content quality. Commercial losses can point to intent mismatch. Losses in one section point to a topic-specific problem." /%}
{% step title="Audit the pages that fell" detail="Compare each page with what ranks now. Check depth, accuracy, first-hand experience and clear authorship. Review links where a link problem is likely." /%}
{% step title="Fix in priority order" detail="Fix technical faults and stated violations first. Then improve existing pages before publishing new ones." /%}
{% step title="Request review or wait for reassessment" detail="A manual action needs a reconsideration request. An algorithmic drop has no request process. Improved pages are reassessed over time." /%}
{% step title="Monitor and adjust" detail="Track rankings and enquiries after each fix. Reassess what is left if the next core update does not help." /%}
{% /process %}

### Confirm the drop is update-related

Confirm the cause before you change anything. Not every drop is caused by an update.

Check [Google's confirmed update history](https://developers.google.com/search/updates/ranking) against the date of your drop. Check in Search Console whether impressions dropped or only clicks. Look for crawl errors, accidental noindex tags or a broken sitemap. These cause drops that look like an update but have nothing to do with Google's ranking systems.

### Identify what fell and why

Export ranking and Search Console data for the weeks around the drop. List the pages and queries that lost ground.

- **Losses mainly on informational queries** often point to content quality, depth or [E-E-A-T](/blog/what-is-eeat-seo/) signals.
- **Losses on commercial queries** can point to intent mismatch: the page no longer matches what Google thinks the searcher wants.
- **Losses in one section while others held** point to a topic-specific problem rather than a site-wide one.

### Audit the content that fell

Ask three questions about each important page that lost rankings. Does it show real expertise and first-hand experience? Does it answer the query as fully as the pages that now outrank it? Is it clear who wrote it and why they can be trusted?

### Check your topical coverage

Check topical coverage by mapping every subtopic your buyers search for. Core updates often favour sites with real topical authority.

Map your content against a full [topical map](/services/topical-maps/) of your subject. Find what is covered well, what is thin and what is missing. Close the biggest gaps first.

### Improve before you scale

The most common recovery mistake is publishing new content before improving existing pages. Google already has data on your current pages. More content of the same quality will not change its view. Update, expand and strengthen the pages that fell first.

### Fix violations and request review (manual actions)

Audit the backlink profile where links are the problem. Attempt removal of unnatural links and disavow the ones you cannot remove. Improve or consolidate thin and duplicate pages where content is the problem. Then submit a reconsideration request in Search Console. Be specific about what you found, what you fixed and how you will stop it recurring. Vague requests get rejected.

## What does not work for recovery

- **Mass content deletion.** It sounds logical but often removes pages that were supporting your topic coverage.
- **Aggressive link building straight after a drop.** Google is assessing quality. A higher link count does not answer that.
- **Reverting to a backup from before the update.** The old content is not what brings recovery.

Avoid these and the other [common SEO mistakes](/blog/seo-mistakes/) that make a drop worse. Improving content quality is the main lever Google's guidance points to. Use it only after technical faults, lost demand and tracking changes have been ruled out.

