## Frequently asked questions

### How long does Google penalty recovery take?

It depends on the cause. A technical fault can recover soon after it is fixed and recrawled. A manual action recovers after Google reviews your reconsideration request.

Core update drops take longer. [Google's guidance on core updates](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/core-updates) says changes can take effect in a few days. It also says its systems could take several months to confirm a site has improved. That could mean waiting until the next core update.

### Can you guarantee recovery after a Google algorithm update?

No. Nobody can guarantee recovery. Google decides how pages are reassessed. What I can do is find the most likely cause, fix it properly and track the result. Some sites need more than one update cycle.

### How do I know if I have a Google penalty or an algorithm drop?

Open Search Console and go to Security & Manual Actions > Manual Actions. A listed issue means you have a manual action. "No issues detected" means the drop is algorithmic, technical or caused by something else. Lost demand and tracking changes are two common examples.

### Do you offer a Google penalty recovery service in the UK?

Yes. I work remotely with sites across the UK. The process is the same wherever you are based. The free diagnosis comes first. The £495 audit or ongoing support follows if you need it.

### Can you recover traffic lost after a site migration?

Yes. Migration drops are usually caused by missing redirects, lost pages or changed templates.

See my [SEO migration consultant](/services/seo-migration-consultant/) service for how I check and repair them.

### Should I disavow links after a drop?

Only when there is a link-related manual action or clear evidence of a link scheme. Disavowing low-quality links that carry no risk does not fix a core update drop.

